mayo.edu> wrote:
> 
>> I have a simple and hopefully not too stupid question: What are the 
>> basic steps for launching and using REngine when you have rJava 
>> installed in R?
>> I've searched the web, and nowhere found a step by step guide on how to 
>> do this.  Is there one somewhere that I've just missed?
>> 
>> A set of instructions would be greatly appreciated.
>> 
>> Thank you,
>> Greg
>> 
>> So far, I have
>> (From the R side)
>> install.packages("rJava")
>> install.packages("Rserve")
>> library(Rserve)
>> 
>>> From the Java side, this works:
>> REngine eng = REngine.engineForClass("org.rosuda.REngine.JRI.JRIEngine");
>> 
>> But then calling this still fails:
>> RConnection c = new RConnection();
>> 
>> Am I no longer supposed to use an RConnection?  Do we now just do everything
>> with REngine?
>> 
> 
> You're mixing two unrelated back-ends:
> 
> Rserve: client/server infrastructure for using R via TCP/IP or sockets
from other systems
> JRI: Java/R interface which allows you to load R into a JVM
> 
> org.rosuda.REngine is a backend-agnostic API that can interface either of
the two above
> 
> org.rosuda.REngine.Rserve.RConnection is a subclass of REngine that use
Rserve.
> org.rosuda.REngine.JRI.JRIEngine is a subclass of REngine that use JRI
> 
> So my first question is - which of the two are you intending to use?
> 
> Cheers,
> Simon
> 
Thank you for clearing that up for me.  I have a servlet that needs to read
a .r file and then execute the contents in R (it will create an image file)

I have enough control over the server environment that I can set up both
Tomcat and R as I wish on the server, so I guess I want to use JRI.

After further work last night, I came up with this:
/**
 * Get the REngine
 * 
 * @return	An REngine, or null if couldn't get one
 */
private static REngine getREngine ()
{
    String[]    dummyArgs = new String[1];
    dummyArgs[0] = "--vanilla";
    REngine eng = null;
    // The one below allows us to see all output from R via 
    // REngineStdOutput()  However, it won't succeed if the 
    // engine doesn't support callbacks, so be prepared to fall back
    try
    {
        eng = REngine.engineForClass ("org.rosuda.REngine.JRI.JRIEngine", 
                                      dummyArgs, new REngineStdOutput (), 
                                      false);
    }
    catch (Exception oops)
    {
        oops.printStackTrace ();
        try
        {
            eng = REngine.engineForClass (
                                "org.rosuda.REngine.JRI.JRIEngine");
        }
        catch (Exception oops2)
        {
            oops.printStackTrace ();
            return null;
        }
    }

    return eng;
}

